                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_9.png" id="1069-0" width=27 height=27 alt="9 polar patterns" title="Cardioid, Omni, Figure 8, and six intermediate positions" ></span>AKG Acoustics C 414 XL II</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Multi-Pattern Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The C414 XL II is a multipattern FET condenser microphone built around AKG&#8217;s famed CK12 capsule. It is the latest in the TLII/XLII series of studio microphones, which feature a presence boost rather than a neutral frequency response. The primary new feature of the revised mic is that it offers 9 polar patterns, rather than the B-XL II&#8217;s five.</p><p><a rel="shadowbox" href="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images//mic_extras/akg/XLII-old-new.jpg"><img src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/mic_extras/akg/XLII-old-new-sm.jpg" class="thumb shaft" alt="C414 B-XLII and XLII" width="133" /></a>Pattern for pattern, the frequency response of the XL II matches that of the B-XL II. The self-noise is the same, 6dBA. The new mic is slightly less sensitive (20mV/Pa) than the B-XL II (23mV/Pa).</p><p>The pattern, pad, and filter are selected via pushbutton controls on the body of the microphone. Current selections are indicated by LEDs. As on the B-XL II, the controls can be locked to prevent accidental reconfiguration during handling.</p><p>The clip indicator LED introduced on the B-XLS and B-XL II is improved on the 2009 models (XLS and XL II), in that the LED stays lit for 3 seconds.</p><p><a rel="shadowbox" href="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images//mic_extras/akg/C414XLS-cutaway.jpg"><img src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/mic_extras/akg/C414XLS-cutaway-sm.jpg" class="thumb shaft" alt="C 414 XLS Cutaway View" width="200" /></a>Like the B-XL II, the new XL II employs surface-mount components within its circuit boards. (Pictured is the XLS, which shares much of the circuitry of the XL II.)</p><p>The amplifier circuit is a low-noise design, making the mic one of the lowest-noise mics we have seen (6dBA in all polar patterns).</p><p><a rel="shadowbox" href="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images//mic_extras/akg/XLII-kit.jpg"><img src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/mic_extras/akg/XLII-kit-sm.jpg" class="thumb shaft" alt="C 414 XLII Kit" width="200" /></a>The C414 XL II ships with a low-profile shockmount (AKG p/n H 85), a foam windscreen (W414), a detachable pop filter (PF80), and an aluminum &#8220;Soundtool&#8221; flight case.</p><p>The C 414 XL II is also available in matched stereo pairs. AKG&#8217;s matching criteria requires the two microphones to produce frequency response within 1dB, between 300Hz&ndash;8000Hz. The stereo kit, AKG p/n C 414 XL II/ST, includes two shockmounts, two windscreens, and a stereo mounting bar, and ships in an oversized Soundtool flightcase. (No pop filter is included.)<p>Permalink: <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/AKG-Acoustics/C-414-XL-II">AKG Acoustics C-414-XL-II</a></p><p>The mic was released in 2009.</p>
